I commented on #1066 and closed it. The other 11 issues got no comment because I did no work on them this session.

#1066 (eval of known env emitters, now closed): I decided to allow it, and that shipped in commit a5d0de3d. With a deny list set, eval "$(ssh-agent)" runs again, but only in one narrow shape:
• eval's only argument is a single substitution containing one simple command (no ;, &&, pipes, quotes, $ or nesting).
• The program is a plain name, not a path, from a short list of env emitters (ssh-agent, direnv, brew shellenv, pyenv and similar), with the first argument it needs.
• The inner command also passes the user's deny globs.

Everything else is still refused. That includes eval "$(printf 'rm -rf x')" and eval "$(ssh-agent; rm -rf x)", and tests pin both. The comment also explains why I'm fine with the issue's two objections:
• If the list is missing an emitter, that emitter is simply refused, so a stale list costs convenience, never safety.
• A fake ssh-agent earlier on PATH would be trusted by name, but the deny check already trusts names for every plain command.

